          "derivation": "Younger Avestan zaŋga-, zaṅgā- m. 'Knöchel (des Fusses)', the ankle or lower leg — Bartholomae col. 1660 — used of ahuric (good) creatures, while the daēvic counterpart is zaŋgra- (col. 1660, 'von daēvischen Wesen'), whence the Avestan compounds bi-zangra- 'two-legged' and caθwarō.zangra- 'four-legged'. It continues *ǵʰéngʰ-o-s (Proto-Indo-Iranian *ȷ́ʰángʰas), with the regular Indo-Iranian palatalisation of PIE *ǵʰ to *ȷ́ʰ and its Iranian outcome z-, exactly as Sanskrit shows the same stem as jaṅghā́ 'lower leg'; the -ng- is the nasal of the root. Nourai's second entry, 'zangô : upper part of the foot', is not a second Avestan lexeme: it is Mann's citation form on IEC p.414, 'Av. zaŋgō \"upper of foot\"', i.e. the nominative singular zaŋgō of the same zaŋga- (Avestan -as > -ō), and his rendering 'upper of foot' is a looser gloss of Bartholomae's 'Knöchel des Fußes'. So the box holds one word in two shapes, and the derivation from the root is textbook.",
